questions.prompt:
The following (HW4) solution checks whether, among the last `indexDiff` elements, some two values differ by at most `valueDiff`.

```cpp
bool containsNearbyAlmostDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int indexDiff, int valueDiff) {
    std::set<int> last_k;
    for (auto it = nums.begin(); it != nums.end(); ++it) {
        auto lb = last_k.lower_bound(*it - valueDiff);
        if ( (lb != last_k.end()) && (*lb <= *it + valueDiff) )
            return true;
        last_k.insert(*it);
        if ( it - nums.begin() >= indexDiff )
            last_k.erase( *(it - indexDiff) );
    }
    return false;
}
```
Let n = `nums.size()` and k = `indexDiff`.

(a) Give the worst-case time complexity as a tight bound, analyzing every call in the loop.
(b) How big does the set get, and why does that matter for the bound?
(c) What would the complexity be if the `erase` line were deleted? Why?
(d) Could an `unordered_set` replace the `set` here? Explain.

questions.answer:
**(a)** The loop runs n times. Per iteration:
* `lower_bound` on a set holding at most k+1 elements: O(log k) (balanced BST descent);
* the boolean test, `end()`, dereference: O(1);
* `insert`: O(log k);
* the index test and the iterator arithmetic: O(1) (vector iterators are random access);
* `erase(value)`: O(log k).
A bunch of O(1) and O(log k) operations in sequence is O(log k) per iteration, n times: **O(n log k)** worst case.

**(b)** The erase keeps the window at the last k elements: after each insertion the set holds at most k+1 elements (then k after the erase). The set never has size n, so every operation is O(log k) and not O(log n). That matters because k can be much smaller than n: n log k is smaller than n log n.

**(c)** Without the `erase` the set keeps growing by one element per iteration (duplicates would have returned true already), reaching size n. Each operation is then O(log of current size), and the last n/2 of them cost at least log(n/2), so the total is **O(n log n)** - tight by the half-the-iterations argument - and the answer would also be wrong, because values outside the index window would still be matched.

**(d)** No. The loop asks for the *nearest value to x in a range*: `lower_bound` returns the smallest element >= x - valueDiff, which needs an **ordered** container. A hash set has no order and no range query (only membership), so it could only test for equality (valueDiff = 0, the 'contains duplicate II' problem), where it gives O(n) average.

