questions.answer:
**Operations needed:** (1) 'have I seen this item before?' once per item; (2) record items in order of first appearance.

**Design.** `unordered_set<string> seen` for (1), `vector<string> out` for (2).
```cpp
vector<string> dedupe(const vector<string>& stream) {
    unordered_set<string> seen;
    vector<string> out;
    for (const string& s : stream)
        if (seen.insert(s).second)   // .second is true only if s was NOT already present
            out.push_back(s);
    return out;
}
```
**Analysis** (string length treated as a constant; otherwise multiply by L):
* per item: `insert` = hash + lookup + possible insert, O(1) average; `push_back` amortized O(1);
* n items: n x O(1) = **O(n) average**; worst case O(n^2) if all items collide into one bucket (each insert then scans O(n) items);
* space: O(u) for the hash and the output, u = number of distinct items.

**Alternatives.**
* `set<string>` instead of the hash: each insert O(log u) (u distinct items so far): **O(n log u)**, in the worst case as well - slower on average but with no worst-case degeneration.
* Sort first (`sort` + `unique`): O(n log n) and the original order is lost; to recover the first-appearance order you must sort (item, index) pairs, remove duplicates keeping the smallest index, then sort by index: O(n log n) overall. More complex and slower than the hash on average.

